  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhanced Humanitarian Assistance and Social Cohesion and Resilience for Crisis-Affected Population</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Niger is affected by crises and recurrent shocks, including drought, epidemics, floods and armed conflicts. As outlined in the 2023 Humanitarian Response Plan (HRP), 4.3 million people, representing about 17 percent of the population, require humanitarian assistance. The Tillab&#xe9;ri, Tahoua, Diffa, and Maradi regions have seen a 14 per cent increase in the number of people requiring humanitarian assistance due to population movements.This project incorporates a two-pronged approach. Firstly, it aims to provide humanitarian assistance in the Diffa, Maradi, and Tillab&#xe9;ri regions, targeting host community, internally displaced persons (IDPs) and returnees affected by either floods or armed conflict. The intervention includes the provision of transitional shelters, non-food item (NFI) kits (including Menstrual Hygiene Management (MHM) kits), income-generating activities, and construction of latrines. Secondly, the project aims to provide a holistic response to the humanitarian situation in Assamaka and the migratory route Assamaka &#x2013; Arlit - Agadez. IOM aims to address challenges faced by migrants and host communities in Assamaka, Arlit, Ingall, and Agadez. The limited social infrastructure exacerbates their vulnerability, resulting in a lack of basic necessities, potential conflicts between migrants and the host communities as well as increased illicit trafficking and insecurity in this fragile border area.</narrative>
  </description>
